<td id="S2.T1.4.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S2.T1.4.5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#CCCCFF;">SAE-Curated</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.4.6" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#CCCCFF;">
<td id="S2.T1.4.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S2.T1.4.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#CCCCFF;">MC-CL<sup id="S2.T1.4.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">⋆</sup></span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.4.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#CCCCFF;" alttext="4.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#CCCCFF;" mathbackground="#CCCCFF" mathsize="0.900em">4.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.7</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.T1.4.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#CCCCFF;">B</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.4.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S2.T1.4.6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#CCCCFF;">SAE-Curated</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.4.7" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#CCCCFF;">
<td id="S2.T1.4.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S2.T1.4.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#CCCCFF;">LLaMa<math id="S2.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#CCCCFF;" mathbackground="#CCCCFF">3.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.1</annotation></semantics></math><sup id="S2.T1.4.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">⋆</sup></span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.4.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="S2.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#CCCCFF;" alttext="6.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#CCCCFF;" mathbackground="#CCCCFF" mathsize="0.900em">6.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.T1.4.7.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#CCCCFF;">B</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.4.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S2.T1.4.7.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#CCCCFF;">SAE-Curated</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2505.00624v1/overall.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/190;" width="476" height="190" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of the FineScope Framework. The method consists of two stages: (1) <span id="S2.F1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Domain-Specific Data Curation</span>, where a Sparse Autoencoder (SAE) is trained on the top-<math id="S2.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> activations of a pretrained LLM and then used to curate a domain-specific dataset from a small set of user-defined seed samples; (2) <span id="S2.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Pruning-Aware Fine-Tuning with Self-Data Distillation</span>, where structured pruning is applied based on the curated dataset, followed by fine-tuning the pruned model using self-data distillation to enhance domain adaptation.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.2 Small Language Models (SLMs). ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> provides an overview of <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FineScope</span>, our proposed framework for domain-specific LLM adaptation. FineScope consists of two key stages: (1) <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Domain-Specific Data Cultivation</span>, where we train a Sparse Autoencoder (SAE) on the Top-<math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> activations of a pretrained LLM and use it to curate a domain-specific dataset based on cosine similarity with a small set of user-defined seed samples; and (2) <span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Pruning-Aware Fine-Tuning with Self-Data Distillation</span>, where structured pruning is applied based on the curated dataset, followed by fine-tuning the pruned model using self-data distillation to further refine task performance. The following sections provide a detailed explanation of each stage.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Domain-Specific Data Cultivation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training SAE: </span>
A Sparse Autoencoder (SAE) is a neural network designed to learn compressed representations of input data while enforcing sparsity constraints on the hidden units <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">EleutherAI, 2024</a>)</cite>. In our framework, SAEs serve as a mechanism for extracting domain-relevant features from pretrained LLM activations to identify domain-relevant samples from a large corpus. Instead of operating directly on the raw model outputs, we train SAEs on activations from intermediate layers of the LLM, allowing us to capture a structured, low-dimensional representation of the underlying knowledge encoded in the model. Additionally, since processing all activations are computationally infeasible for the large corpus, our SAEs is adapted to learn only the representative activations in a way that highlights the most significant neurons, improving interpretability while discarding less relevant signals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In decoder-only transformer models such as GPT-2 and LLaMa, the activation flow can be formally defined as follows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Braun et al., 2024</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="act^{(l)}(x)=f^{(l)}\left(act^{(l-1)}(x)\right),\text{for }l=1,\dots,L-1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext>for </mtext><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">act^{(l)}(x)=f^{(l)}\left(act^{(l-1)}(x)\right),\text{for }l=1,\dots,L-1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="act^{(l)}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">act^{(l)}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the activations at layer <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> given input <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the transformation function at layer <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, which typically includes multi-head self-attention, feed-forward operations, and residual connections. The final model output is computed as:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=\text{softmax}\left(f^{(L)}\left(act^{(L-1)}(x)\right)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=\text{softmax}\left(f^{(L)}\left(act^{(L-1)}(x)\right)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To train the SAE, we extract activations <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="act^{(l)}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">act^{(l)}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> from a selected layer of the pretrained LLM and feed them into the encoder network:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Enc}\left(act^{(l)}(x)\right)=\text{ReLU}\left(W_{e}act^{(l)}(x)+b_{e}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Enc</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>ReLU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Enc}\left(act^{(l)}(x)\right)=\text{ReLU}\left(W_{e}act^{(l)}(x)+b_{e}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The corresponding reconstruction from the decoder is given by:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SAE}\left(act^{(l)}(x)\right)=D^{\top}\text{Enc}\left(act^{(l)}(x)\right)+b_{d}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>SAE</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>Enc</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SAE}\left(act^{(l)}(x)\right)=D^{\top}\text{Enc}\left(act^{(l)}(x)\right)+b_{d}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <span id="S3.SS1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Enc</span> represents the SAE’s encoder, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> are the encoder’s weights and biases, and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to the decoder parameters. The SAE is trained to minimize the reconstruction loss:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{L}_{\text{SAE}}&amp;=\|\text{SAE}(act^{(l)}(x))-act^{(l)}(x)\|^{2}\\
&amp;\quad+\lambda\|\text{Enc}(act^{(l)}(x))\|_{1},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>SAE</mtext></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>SAE</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mtext>Enc</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{L}_{\text{SAE}}&amp;=\|\text{SAE}(act^{(l)}(x))-act^{(l)}(x)\|^{2}\\
&amp;\quad+\lambda\|\text{Enc}(act^{(l)}(x))\|_{1},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where the second term enforces sparsity by penalizing the activation magnitudes of the encoder.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top-K Activation Selection for Efficient SAE Training: </span>
Rather than training the SAE on full-layer activations, we apply a <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Top-K filtering mechanism</span> to select the most important activations before feeding them into the encoder. Given dataset <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> samples, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\{x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\{x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> most significant activations based on the gradient magnitude:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{TopK}(act^{(l)}(x),K)=\{a_{i}\in act^{(l)}(x)\mid\\
a_{i}\text{ corresponds to one of the }K\text{ largest }\left|\frac{\partial act^{(l)}_{i}(x)}{\partial x}\right|\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>TopK</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">∣</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext> corresponds to one of the </mtext><mi>K</mi><mtext> largest </mtext><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{TopK}(act^{(l)}(x),K)=\{a_{i}\in act^{(l)}(x)\mid\\
a_{i}\text{ corresponds to one of the }K\text{ largest }\left|\frac{\partial act^{(l)}_{i}(x)}{\partial x}\right|\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Using only the <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TopK" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TopK</annotation></semantics></math> activations reduces the input dimensionality of the SAE, significantly lowering computational overhead and enabling efficient training on large-scale corpora. Additionally, filtering out noisy activations improves generalization by focusing the SAE on the most informative neurons, enhancing interpretability of extracted features aligned with domain-relevant information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The encoder then operates on these filtered activations as:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Enc}\left(\text{TopK}(act^{(l)}(x),K)\right)=\\
\text{ReLU}\left(W_{e}\cdot\text{TopK}(act^{(l)}(x),K)+b_{e}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Enc</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mtext>TopK</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mtext>ReLU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>TopK</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Enc}\left(\text{TopK}(act^{(l)}(x),K)\right)=\\
\text{ReLU}\left(W_{e}\cdot\text{TopK}(act^{(l)}(x),K)+b_{e}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">For each layer <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> in the LLM, we train a separate SAE, resulting in a total of <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> SAEs. Once trained, these SAEs serve as <span id="S3.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">feature extractors for data curation</span>, which we describe in the next section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset Curation: </span> Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Domain-Specific Data Cultivation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> provides an overview of our dataset curation process using trained SAEs. Given a <span id="S3.SS1.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">small number of seed samples for a target domain</span> (e.g., around ten samples), our goal is to extract a subset <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{s}\subseteq U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{s}\subseteq U</annotation></semantics></math> from a larger mixed-domain dataset <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> by selecting samples that are most similar to the seed samples <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in the learned embedding space.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2505.00624v1/Figures/init_rev_.jpg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/145;" width="476" height="145" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Dataset curation using SAE-based embeddings and cosine similarity.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Using the trained SAE, we compute embeddings for all samples in both <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. The embeddings for the target domain are given by <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}=\{\text{SAE}(x)\mid x\in D_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>SAE</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}=\{\text{SAE}(x)\mid x\in D_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, we compute embeddings for all samples in the larger dataset <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{U}=\{\text{SAE}(x)\mid x\in U\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>SAE</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{U}=\{\text{SAE}(x)\mid x\in U\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">To identify samples in <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> that are most similar to <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the cosine similarity between each embedding <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{u}\in E_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{u}\in E_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and every embedding <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{t}\in E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{t}\in E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using this similarity measure, we select samples <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{u}\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{u}\in U</annotation></semantics></math> whose embeddings fall within the Top-K highest cosine similarity scores when compared to the embeddings in <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, we define the selected dataset <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}D_{s}=\{x_{u}\in U\mid e_{u}\in\\
\text{TopK}(\{\text{CosSim}(\text{SAE}(x_{u}),e_{t}):e_{t}\in E_{t}\})\}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mtext>TopK</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext>CosSim</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>SAE</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}D_{s}=\{x_{u}\in U\mid e_{u}\in\\
\text{TopK}(\{\text{CosSim}(\text{SAE}(x_{u}),e_{t}):e_{t}\in E_{t}\})\}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p12.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CosSim}(e_{u},e_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>CosSim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CosSim}(e_{u},e_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the cosine similarity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">The final dataset <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> contains samples from <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> that are most semantically similar to the target domain <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the cosine similarity of their SAE embeddings. To ensure that <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> remains a high-quality domain-specific dataset, we also apply a similarity threshold <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> to exclude samples with low similarity scores and remove duplicate and misclassified samples to prevent redundancy and noise. In our evaluation, the value of <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> across all SAEs is set to <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> for consistency in selection.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Pruning and Fine-Tuning with Self-Data Distillation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pruning: </span> To improve model efficiency while preserving domain-specific knowledge, we apply structured pruning to remove less significant parameters. Instead of performing global pruning, we identify and retain the most active components of the model based on their contribution to the domain-specific dataset <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. This ensures that pruning preserves knowledge relevant to the target domain while discarding less useful parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We leverage LLM-Pruner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, which performs structured block-wise pruning to remove redundant model components while maintaining computational efficiency. This approach ensures that pruned models retain domain-relevant representations while significantly reducing inference cost.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, let <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the full model and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(D_{s},m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(D_{s},m)</annotation></semantics></math> denote an activity function that quantifies the contribution of each component <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to the dataset <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the pruned model <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{r}=\{m\in\mathcal{M}\mid\mathcal{A}(D_{s},m)&gt;\tau\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{r}=\{m\in\mathcal{M}\mid\mathcal{A}(D_{s},m)&gt;\tau\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is a predefined threshold determining which components are retained. The pruning ratio, denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, is treated as a hyperparameter and optimized to balance model size and performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-Data Distillation and Fine-Tuning: </span> Self-Data Distillation (SDFT) is a technique where a distilled dataset is generated to match the output distribution of the original model, effectively improving generalization. While SDFT is effective for knowledge transfer, its role in FineScope extends beyond dataset refinement. Since pruning removes a significant portion of the model’s parameters, it risks discarding essential representations that contribute to domain-specific knowledge. To mitigate this, we use self-data distillation to recover lost information and reinforce the pruned model by reintroducing high-confidence outputs from a stronger/unpruned teacher model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The pruned model may lose critical task-relevant features due to parameter reduction. Training on self-distilled outputs helps restore <span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">soft knowledge</span> from the teacher model, ensuring that the pruned model retains domain-specific patterns without requiring additional labeled data. Furthermore, unlike standard fine-tuning, which adapts to new domains based solely on curated datasets, self-data distillation smooths the learning process by preserving consistency in the model’s output distribution. This reduces <span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">overfitting to the smaller curated dataset</span> while maintaining alignment with the original domain representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In our framework, we adapt SDFT to further refine the pruned model. Specifically, we use either (i) the original model (for Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.1 Models ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) or (ii) a pretrained state-of-the-art model (for Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.1 Models ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) to generate the distilled dataset. This dataset is then used to fine-tune the pruned model, ensuring that it retains key domain-specific knowledge while benefiting from improved generalization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In the original SDFT proposal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, for an input <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, context <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and output <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from the teacher model, the generated (distilled) output is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}\sim f(y\mid c^{t},x^{t},y^{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}\sim f(y\mid c^{t},x^{t},y^{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, the modified loss function in the fine-tuning procedure of our proposed FineScope framework is:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{msdft}}=-\log f_{p}(y^{\prime}\mid c^{t},y^{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>msdft</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{msdft}}=-\log f_{p}(y^{\prime}\mid c^{t},y^{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is the pruned model and <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{msdft}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>msdft</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{msdft}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the self-distillation loss.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Evaluation</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Models</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our method using three models: Vicuna-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, a fine-tuned version of LLaMa 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>; MathCoder-CL-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, a CodeLlama <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roziere et al., 2023</a>)</cite> variant; and LLaMa 3.1-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.2 Small Language Models (SLMs). ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes these models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Summary of experimental setup for each table</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
