</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate FineScope under four experimental settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(1) Domain-Specific Tuning</span> We prune models using our SAE-curated domain-specific dataset (Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Domain-Specific Data Cultivation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>). SAEs are trained on the RedPajama dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Computer, 2023</a>)</cite>, which includes data from CommonCrawl, C4, GitHub, Wikipedia, Books3, ArXiv, and StackExchange, ensuring generalization across multiple domains. Using these SAEs, we curate a domain-specific dataset from OpenInstruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">hakurei, 2023</a>)</cite>, which comprises Alpaca <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lan, 2019</a>)</cite>, Self-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, GPT-4 Instruct, Roleplay <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Teknium, 2023</a>)</cite>, Code Alpaca <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chaudhary, 2023</a>)</cite>, Dolly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and other instruction datasets. We extract STEM, Social Sciences, and Humanities subsets, each curated from <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">15</span> GPT-4o-generated seed samples, where each curated dataset was augmented using Grok API <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">xAI, 2023</a>)</cite>. The final curated dataset sizes are 2,703 (STEM), 3,327 (Social Sciences), and 3,522 (Humanities).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(2) Baseline Tuning</span> We compare against two baselines: (a) Fine-tuning pretrained models with SAE-curated datasets. (b) Pruning the model and fine-tuning it with Alpaca data, using the same pruning strategy as in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Pruning and Fine-Tuning with Self-Data Distillation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(3) Baseline (Pretrained)</span> Pretrained models are evaluated without fine-tuning, serving as a baseline. We compare against GPT-3 (6.7B, 175B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and OLMO-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Groeneveld et al., 2024</a>)</cite> (Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.1 Models ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), as well as GPT-3 (13B, 175B) (Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.1 Models ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(4) Sub-Domain Tuning (Math)</span> To evaluate fine-tuning at a more granular level, we curate sub-domains within Math. We train SAEs on MetaMath <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and curate datasets from the Math dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021c</a>)</cite>. Unlike previous settings, all Math sub-domains are merged into a unified dataset. After curation, we extract Pre-Algebra, Algebra, and Counting &amp; Probability subsets. Models are tuned using OpenMath2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Toshniwal et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, while Notus-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tunstall et al., 2023</a>)</cite> is used for Alpaca-based tuning. Evaluations are performed separately for each sub-domain (Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.1 Models ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For quantifiable evaluation, we utlize MMLU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021a</a>)</cite> and Math test set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021c</a>)</cite>, using lm-evaluation-harness (MMLU) and bigcode-evaluation-harness (Math). We present two primary results tables: (i) Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.1 Models ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> compares settings (1), (2a, 2b), (3), and SOTA models, and (i) Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.1 Models ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> evaluates settings (2b), (3), and (4). A summary of experimental settings is provided in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1 Models ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.1 Models ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents the evaluation results on the MMLU dataset across three domains. The results show that the domain-specialized pruned models, which are fine-tuned by FineScope with our SAE-curated domain-specific dataset, improve average performance over Alpaca tuning by 4.13% (STEM), 2.25% (Social Sciences), and 5.40% (Humanities) across all models. Among the three evaluated models, MathCoder-CL shows the highest improvement (+16.71), benefiting from exposure to a more updated dataset, followed by LLaMa 3.1 (+2.41) and Vicuna (+1.2). This demonstrates that SAE-curated datasets effectively enhance domain-specific adaptation without significant human effort.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is noteworthy that, after pruning, model performance significantly degrades, e.g., by 50% (Vicuna), 48% (MathCoder-CL), and 35% (LLaMa 3.1) on average across all domains. LLaMa 3.1 exhibits the smallest performance drop, likely due to its more balanced initial performance and domain-aware pruning, which retains a larger proportion of essential features.
Compared to GPT-3 (6.7B, 175B) and OLMO-7B, our method outperforms in most cases even though our pruned models have a small number of parameters. However, GPT-3 surpasses our method in Social Sciences, and GPT-3 (175B) outperforms in Humanities, while our tuned models consistently outperform OLMO-7B.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.1 Models ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows performance on Math-based domains, where our domain-specific tuning significantly improves results. Compared to Alpaca-tuned models, performance gains are +7.50 (Vicuna), +8.15 (MathCoder-CL), and +18.70 (LLaMa 3.1). Notably, pruning causes severe degradation for Vicuna and MathCoder-CL, and Alpaca tuning does not fully recover their lost performance due to a lack of math-specific data. LLaMa 3.1 also performs worse when tuned with Alpaca, likely due to catastrophic forgetting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">When compared to GPT models, our tuned models achieve competitive results, with notable differences likely stemming from imbalanced training datasets in GPT models, which may limit their generalization across diverse math domains.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Ablative Study</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pruning Ratio:</span> Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.4 Ablative Study ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> presents the effect of pruning ratios on model performance. Using Vicuna as a case study, we evaluate domain-specific tuning under different pruning ratios. The results confirm that higher pruning ratios lead to progressively larger performance drops, reinforcing the importance of selecting an optimal pruning threshold. The results present that models with 50% and 70% pruning exhibit significant performance degradation compared to those with 30% pruning. Conversely, pruning only 10% of parameters yields the best performance; however, the model remains computationally expensive due to its large parameter count. To balance efficiency and performance, we select a 30% pruning ratio, which achieves a favorable trade-off between model size and accuracy.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Comparative performance analysis of pruned models with different pruning ratio. <span id="S4.T5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PR(%)</span> : Pruning ratio, <span id="S4.T5.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Params (B)</span> : Number of parameters in Billions, <span id="S4.T5.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">STEM</span> : Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics; <span id="S4.T5.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SS</span> : Social Sciences; <span id="S4.T5.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hum</span> : Humanities (MMLU)</figcaption>
