<th id="S4.T5.12.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="S4.T5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T5.12.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">3.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.6</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T5.12.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">20.75</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20.75</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T5.12.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24.33" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">24.33</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24.33</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T5.12.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25.91" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">25.91</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25.91</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T5.12.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T5.12.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"></th>
<th id="S4.T5.12.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">70</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T5.12.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">2.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.4</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T5.12.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10.10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">10.10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10.10</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T5.12.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.82" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">9.82</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.82</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T5.12.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8.21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">8.21</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8.21</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SAE’s <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TopK" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TopK</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> To determine the optimal value of <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, we trained SAEs with varying <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> values and monitored reconstruction loss, balancing computational efficiency and training quality. Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.4 Ablative Study ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the impact of <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> on reconstruction loss. For demonstration, we have chosen three of initial, middle, and final layers. Even though the larger <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> values curate more samples to train inituitively, there is no much performance difference for <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K&gt;96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>96</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K&gt;96</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we select <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>96</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=96</annotation></semantics></math>, which provides a balanced trade-off, in our evaluation. The results for additional layers are available in the appendix.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2505.00624v1/topk.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/375;" width="476" height="375" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Effect of <math id="S4.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> on reconstruction loss</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Seed Selection:</span>
We visualize the seed selection process using cluster analysis, as shown in <span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.4 Ablative Study ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a></span>. The interpretation of features varies across initial, middle, and final layers, reflecting how representations evolve within the model. The results suggest that our curated dataset is alinged with the clustered virtual domain target domains across different layer representations. Lower layers tend to capture broad, generalized features, often encoding syntactic structures or common linguistic patterns. In contrast, deeper layers focus on increasingly abstract and domain-specific attributes, leading to more compact and semantically meaningful clusters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This progressive refinement suggests that domain-relevant information emerges more distinctly in later layers, where feature representations become more specialized. Our method leverages this hierarchical structure, selecting seeds from layers that balance generalization and domain specificity. By utilizing SAE-based representations instead of raw embeddings, we ensure that the seed selection process is more interpretable and aligned with high-level domain knowledge, rather than being influenced by superficial token-level similarities. This reinforces the effectiveness of our approach in identifying the most informative seed samples for dataset curation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2505.00624v1/Figures/cluster.jpg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/335;" width="476" height="335" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Cluster visualization for seed selection for dataset curation (for STEM)</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced FineScope, a framework for developing domain-specific models through SAE-guided dataset curation, domain-aware pruning, and self-distilled fine-tuning. FineScope addresses a key challenge in domain adaptation: how to efficiently construct high-quality domain-specific datasets while maintaining computational efficiency in fine-tuning LLMs. Our approach consists of three key steps: (1) Training an SAE to learn compressed domain representations, (2) Using the trained SAE to curate a domain-specific dataset from a large corpus, and (3) Applying domain-aware pruning followed by self-data distillation to recover lost knowledge and enhance fine-tuning stability. FineScope ensures that models retain domain-relevant features while significantly reducing computational cost.
Experiments demonstrate that FineScope outperforms state-of-the-art methods in most cases. Our approach helps restore pruned models’ performance while significantly enhancing domain-specific adaptation. Additionally, tuning pretrained models with SAE-curated datasets enables them to surpass original models in domain-specific tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
