</section>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Supplementary</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Seed Selection</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A1.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ A.1 Seed Selection ‣ Appendix A Supplementary ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> demonstrates the seed selection process for all the blocks in the LLM. From the figure, we can observe how the later layers make the selected seeds more dense compared to the initial layers. In initial layers, the selected seeds are more spread along the clusters and seem more generalized whereas the later layers are more domain-specific.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2505.00624v1/Figures/cluster_exte.jpg" id="A1.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:381/490;" width="381" height="490" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Extended visualization of Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.4 Ablative Study ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Effects of <math id="A1.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> on Reconstruction Loss</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the impact of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, we conducted experiments varying its value and analyzed the corresponding reconstruction loss. <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Figure <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ A.2 Effects of 𝐾 on Reconstruction Loss ‣ Appendix A Supplementary ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a></span> provides an extended visualization to <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.4 Ablative Study ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></span>, illustrating how the initial reconstruction loss fluctuates across different layers and blocks. Based on the results, we determined <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>96</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=96</annotation></semantics></math> which provides the most balanced trade-off between information retention and computational efficiency. At lower values of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, the SAE discards too many activations, leading to a loss of critical semantic features necessary for high-quality dataset curation. Conversely, excessively high values of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> retain activations that contribute little additional information, increasing computational cost without significant gains in representation quality. This trend is consistent across all evaluated layers, reinforcing that <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>96</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=96</annotation></semantics></math> offers an optimal balance for our domain-specific model adaptation task.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2505.00624v1/Figures/topk_extended.jpg" id="A1.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/483;" width="476" height="483" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Extended visualization of Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.4 Ablative Study ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ FineScope : Precision Pruning for Domain-Specialized Large Language Models Using SAE-Guided Self-Data Cultivation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
